D3 red. Implement the guard: one flip per axis per pass. Restructure the brick loop into two phases:
1. Collect hits (all overlapping alive bricks) → destroy + score all.
2. Flip each axis at most once (based on the smallest-penetration face per hit) + resolve.

Wait — but D3's assertions also need the resolution to push the ball to y = 93 (brick.y - r). The push after the guard: the flip axis is y (both hits are top faces). Push y = 93.

Hmm — also the current per-brick loop destroys+flips inline. Let me restructure:

```js
// Bricks (pure logic): detect all hits of this pass first, then a single
// bounce per axis (two flips on a grid seam would cancel and let the ball
// plow through — see playtest D3).
const hits = [];
for (const brick of state.bricks) {
  if (!brick.alive) continue;
  if (!collides(ball, brick)) continue;
  brick.alive = false;
  state.combo += 1;
  state.score += scoreForBrick(brick, state.combo);
  hits.push(brick);
}
let flippedVx = false;
let flippedVy = false;
let bounceX = null;   // target x after the bounce (or null: no x bounce)
let bounceY = null;   // target y after the bounce
for (const brick of hits) {
  const overlapX = Math.min(ball.x + ball.r, brick.x + brick.w) - Math.max(ball.x - ball.r, brick.x);
  const overlapY = Math.min(ball.y + ball.r, brick.y + brick.h) - Math.max(ball.y - ball.r, brick.y);
  if (overlapX < overlapY) {
    if (!flippedVx) {
      ball.vx = -ball.vx;
      flippedVx = true;
      bounceX = ball.vx < 0 ? brick.x - ball.r : brick.x + brick.w + ball.r;
    }
  } else if (!flippedVy) {
    ball.vy = -ball.vy;
    flippedVy = true;
    bounceY = ball.vy < 0 ? brick.y - ball.r : brick.y + brick.h + ball.r;
  }
}
if (bounceX !== null) ball.x = bounceX;
if (bounceY !== null) ball.y = bounceY;
```

Wait — the D3 case: both hits have overlapX = 3 > overlapY = 1.33 → both go to the else branch → flip vy once (guard) ✓, bounceY = 93 ✓. vx untouched ✓. Ball.x untouched (320) ✓. Assert vy < 0 ✓, vx == 0 ✓, ball.y == 93 ✓, both bricks dead ✓ (destroyed in pass 1), score 35 ✓ (combo order preserved: A then B).

Hmm — but careful: for the D3 case, the SECOND brick B: overlapX = 3, overlapY = 1.33 → else branch → `if (!flippedVy)` → false → skip ✓.

And D4 (corner) will come later — currently the tie goes to the else (vy) branch (overlapX < overlapY false). That's fine for now (D4 red until its fix).

Hmm wait — one concern: with the guard, is D1's flip still per-axis-correct? D1a: one hit → flip vy ✓. D1b: one hit → flip vx ✓. D2: same ✓. ✓

Let me also double check the multi-hit bounceX/bounceY: for the seam case, only bounceY set. For a hypothetical mixed case, both set. OK.

Now — is there a subtle issue with `bounceX = ball.vx < 0 ? ...` when the velocity was just flipped? ball.vx after flip: if it was +260 → -260 → < 0 → brick.x - r (left) ✓ (came from left). If it was -260 → +260 → brick.x + w + r ✓. Correct.

